  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-habrosus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The chessboard dwarf cory, Corydoras habrosus, grows to a maximum of 4 cm. It is completely peaceful and leaves plants alone, so it is very suitable for community aquariums.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-habrosus-2/",
      "html": "<p>C. habrosus originates from the Orinoco drainage (Colombia and Venezuela).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-habrosus-2/",
      "html": "<p>The usual care recommendations for Corydoras apply: they should be kept in groups and at least some areas of the bottom should be covered with fine sand.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-habrosus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Since it is very warm in the Orinoco drainage, the water temperature should be somewhat higher than usual: 26-30°C.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/corydoras-habrosus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Any usual fish food is eaten, as long as it fits into the little mouth.</p>"
    }
  ],
